#show: template.with(title: "The Economics of the Coming Spaceship Earth", author: "Kenneth E. Boulding", date: "1966", lang: "en", css: ("/assets/custom.css", "/assets/essay.css"))

= The Economics of the Coming Spaceship Earth / 即将到来的宇宙飞船地球的经济学

#html.p(id: "en-S001")[#text("We are now in the middle of a long process of transition in the nature of the image which man has of himself and his environment. Primitive men, and to a large extent also men of the early civilizations, imagined themselves to be living on a virtually illimitable plane. There was almost always somewhere beyond the known limits of human habitation, and over a very large part of the time that man has been on earth, there has been something like a frontier. That is, there was always some place else to go when things got too difficult, either by reason of the deterioration of the natural environment or a deterioration of the social structure in places where people happened to live. The image of the frontier is probably one of the oldest images of mankind, and it is not surprising that we find it hard to get rid of.")]

#zh(id: "S001")[#text("我们现在正处于人类对自己和环境的形象本质上的一个漫长的转变过程之中。原始人，在很大程度上也是早期文明中的人，想象自己生活在一个几乎无限的平面上。几乎总是存在着超出人类居住已知限制的地方，并且在人类存在于地球上的很大一部分时间里，存在着类似于边境的东西。也就是说，当事情变得太困难时，无论是由于自然环境的恶化还是人们碰巧居住的地方的社会结构的恶化，总有其他地方可以去。边疆的形象可能是人类最古老的形象之一，我们很难摆脱它也就不足为奇了。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S002")[#text("Gradually, however, man has been accustoming himself to the notion of the spherical earth and a closed sphere of human activity. A few unusual spirits among the ancient Greeks perceived that the earth was a sphere. It was only with the circumnavigations and the geographical explorations of the fifteenth and sixteenth centuries, however, that the fact that the earth was a sphere became at all widely known and accepted. Even in the nineteenth century, the commonest map was Mercator's projection, which visualizes the earth as an illimitable cylinder, essentially a plane wrapped around the globe, and it was not until the Second World War and the development of the air age that the global nature of the planet really entered the popular imagination. Even now we are very far from having made the moral, political, and psychological adjustments which are implied in this transition from the illimitable plane to the closed sphere.")]

#zh(id: "S002")[#text("然而，人类逐渐习惯了球形地球和人类活动的封闭范围的概念。古希腊人中一些不寻常的人认为地球是一个球体。然而，直到十五世纪和十六世纪的环球航行和地理探索之后，地球是一个球体的事实才被广泛认识和接受。即使在十九世纪，最常见的地图也是墨卡托投影，它将地球形象化为一个无限的圆柱体，本质上是一个环绕地球的平面，直到第二次世界大战和航空时代的发展，地球的全球性质才真正进入大众的想象。即使现在，我们还远远没有做出从无限平面到封闭球体的转变所隐含的道德、政治和心理调整。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S003")[#text("Economists in particular, for the most part, have failed to come to grips with the ultimate consequences of the transition from the open to the closed earth. One hesitates to use the terms \"open\" and \"closed\" in this connection, as they have been used with so many different shades of meaning. Nevertheless, it is hard to find equivalents. The open system, indeed, has some similarities to the open system of von Bertalanffy, in that it implies that some kind of a structure is maintained in the midst of a throughput from inputs to outputs.[1] In a closed system, the outputs of all parts of the system are linked to the inputs of other parts. There are no inputs from outside and no outputs to the outside; indeed, there is no outside at all. Closed systems, in fact, are very rare in human experience, in fact almost by definition unknowable, for if there are genuinely closed systems around us, we have no way of getting information into them or out of them; and hence if they are really closed, we would be quite unaware of their existence. We can only find out about a closed system if we participate in it. Some isolated primitive societies may have approximated to this, but even these had to take inputs from the environment and give outputs to it. All living organisms, including man himself, are open systems. They have to receive inputs in the shape of air, food, water, and give off outputs in the form of effluvia and excrement. Deprivation of input of air, even for a few minutes, is fatal. Deprivation of the ability to obtain any input or to dispose of any output is fatal in a relatively short time. All human societies have likewise been open systems. They receive inputs from the earth, the atmosphere, and the waters, and they give outputs into these reservoirs; they also produce inputs internally in the shape of babies and outputs in the shape of corpses. Given a capacity to draw upon inputs and to get rid of outputs, an open system of this kind can persist indefinitely.")]

#zh(id: "S003")[#text("尤其是经济学家，在很大程度上未能正确认识地球从开放向封闭转变的最终后果。人们在这方面犹豫是否使用术语“开放”和“封闭”，因为它们已被用于具有多种不同含义的含义。然而，很难找到同等的东西。事实上，开放系统与冯·贝塔朗菲的开放系统有一些相似之处，因为它意味着在从输入到输出的吞吐量中维护着某种结构。 [1]在封闭系统中，系统所有部分的输出都链接到其他部分的输入。没有来自外部的输入，也没有向外部的输出；事实上，根本就没有外面的东西。事实上，封闭系统在人类经验中非常罕见，实际上从定义上来说几乎是不可知的，因为如果我们周围存在真正的封闭系统，我们就无法将信息输入或输出；因此，如果它们真的关闭，我们将完全不知道它们的存在。只有参与其中，我们才能了解一个封闭的系统。一些孤立的原始社会可能已经接近这一点，但即使这些社会也必须从环境中获取输入并向其提供输出。所有生物体，包括人类本身，都是开放系统。它们必须以空气、食物、水的形式接受输入，并以恶臭和排泄物的形式排出输出。缺乏空气输入，即使是几分钟，也是致命的。在相对较短的时间内剥夺获得任何输入或处理任何输出的能力是致命的。所有人类社会同样都是开放系统。它们从地球、大气和水域接收输入，并将输出输入这些水库；他们还在内部产生婴儿形状的输入和尸体形状的输出。如果有能力利用输入并摆脱输出，这种开放系统可以无限期地持续下去。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S004")[#text("There are some systems - such as the biological phenotype, for instance the human body - which cannot maintain themselves indefinitely by inputs and outputs because of the phenomenon of aging. This process is very little understood. It occurs, evidently, because there are some outputs which cannot be replaced by any known input. There is not the same necessity for aging in organizations and in societies, although an analogous phenomenon may take place. The structure and composition of an organization or society, however, can be maintained by inputs of fresh personnel from birth and education as the existing personnel ages and eventually dies. Here we have an interesting example of a system which seems to maintain itself by the self-generation of inputs, and in this sense is moving towards closure. The input of people (that is, babies) is also an output of people (that is, parents).")]

#zh(id: "S004")[#text("有一些系统——例如生物表型，例如人体——由于衰老现象而无法通过输入和输出无限期地维持自身。人们对这个过程知之甚少。显然，这种情况的发生是因为有些输出无法用任何已知的输入来替代。尽管可能会发生类似的现象，但组织和社会中的老龄化并不具有同样的必要性。然而，随着现有人员的老化和最终死亡，组织或社会的结构和组成可以通过出生和教育的新人员的投入来维持。这里我们有一个有趣的例子，系统似乎通过自我生成输入来维持自身，从这个意义上说，它正在走向封闭。人（即婴儿）的输入也是人（即父母）的输出。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S005")[#text("Systems may be open or closed in respect to a number of classes of inputs and outputs. Three important classes are matter, energy, and information. The present world economy is open in regard to all three. We can think of the world economy or \"econosphere\" as a subset of the \"world set,\" which is the set of all objects of possible discourse in the world. We then think of the state of the econosphere at anyone moment as being the total capital stock, that is, the set of all objects, people, organizations, and so on, which are interesting from the point of view of the system of exchange. This total stock of capital is clearly an open system in the sense that it has inputs and outputs, inputs being production which adds to the capital stock, outputs being consumption which subtracts from it. From a material point of view, we see objects passing from the noneconomic into the economic set in the process of production, and we similarly see products passing out of the economic set as their value becomes zero. Thus we see the econosphere as a material process involving the discovery and mining of fossil fuels, ores, etc., and at the other end a process by which the effluents of the system are passed out into noneconomic reservoirs - for instance, the atmosphere and the oceans - which are not appropriated and do not enter into the exchange system.")]

#zh(id: "S005")[#text("系统对于许多类别的输入和输出可以是开放的或封闭的。三个重要的类别是物质、能量和信息。当前的世界经济在这三个方面都是开放的。我们可以将世界经济或“经济圈”视为“世界集合”的子集，“世界集合”是世界上所有可能讨论的对象的集合。然后，我们将经济圈在任何时刻的状态视为总资本存量，即所有物体、人、组织等的集合，从交换体系的角度来看，这是有趣的。资本总存量显然是一个开放系统，因为它有投入和产出，投入是增加资本存量的生产，产出是减少资本存量的消费。从物质的角度来看，我们看到物体在生产过程中从非经济集合进入经济集​​合，同样我们看到产品随着其价值变为零而从经济集合中转移出来。因此，我们将经济圈视为一个涉及化石燃料、矿石等的发现和开采的物质过程，而在另一端，系统的流出物被排入非经济水库（例如大气和海洋）的过程，这些水库没有被占用，也没有进入交换系统。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S006")[#text("From the point of view of the energy system, the econosphere involves inputs of available energy in the form, say, of water power, fossil fuels, or sunlight, which are necessary in order to create the material throughput and to move matter from the noneconomic set into the economic set or even out of it again; and energy itself is given off by the system in a less available form, mostly in the form of heat. These inputs of available energy must come either from the sun (the energy supplied by other stars being assumed to be negligible) or it may come from the earth itself, either through its internal heat or through its energy of rotation or other motions, which generate, for instance, the energy of the tides. Agriculture, a few solar machines, and water power use the current available energy income. In advanced societies this is supplemented very extensively by the use of fossil fuels, which represent as it were a capital stock of stored-up sunshine. Because of this capital stock of energy, we have been able to maintain an energy input into the system, particularly over the last two centuries, much larger than we would have been able to do with existing techniques if we had had to rely on the current input of available energy from the sun or the earth itself. This supplementary input, however, is by its very nature exhaustible. The inputs and outputs of information are more subtle and harder to trace, but also represent an open system, related to, but not wholly dependent on, the transformations of matter and energy. By far the larger amount of information and knowledge is self-generated by the human society, though a certain amount of information comes into the sociosphere in the form of light from the universe outside. The information that comes from the universe has certainly affected man's image of himself and of his environment, as we can easily visualize if we suppose that we lived on a planet with a total cloud-cover that kept out all information from the exterior universe. It is only in very recent times, of course, that the information coming in from the universe has been captured and coded into the form of a complex image of what the universe is like outside the earth; but even in primitive times, man's perception of the heavenly bodies has always profoundly affected his image of earth and of himself. It is the information generated within the planet, however, and particularly that generated by man himself, which forms by far the larger part of the information system. We can think of the stock of knowledge, or as Teilhard de Chardin called it, the \"noosphere,\" and consider this as an open system, losing knowledge through aging and death and gaining it through birth and education and the ordinary experience of life.")]

#zh(id: "S006")[#text("从能源系统的角度来看，经济圈涉及以水力、化石燃料或阳光等形式提供的可用能源的输入，这些输入对于创造物质吞吐量以及将物质从非经济集合转移到经济集合甚至再次从经济集合中转移是必要的；系统本身以一种不太可用的形式释放能量，主要以热量的形式。这些可用能量的输入必须来自太阳（假设其他恒星提供的能量可以忽略不计），也可能来自地球本身，要么通过其内部热量，要么通过其旋转或其他运动的能量，例如产生潮汐能量。农业、一些太阳能设备和水力发电使用当前可用的能源收入。在先进社会中，化石燃料的使用广泛地补充了这一点，化石燃料代表着储存的阳光的资本存量。由于这种能量资本存量，我们能够维持系统的能量输入，特别是在过去两个世纪中，如果我们不得不依赖来自太阳或地球本身的可用能量的当前输入，那么我们使用现有技术所能做到的能量输入要大得多。然而，这种补充投入本质上是用尽的。信息的输入和输出更加微妙，更难以追踪，但也代表了一个开放的系统，与物质和能量的转换相关，但并不完全依赖于物质和能量的转换。到目前为止，大量的信息和知识是人类社会自身产生的，尽管一定数量的信息以光的形式从外部宇宙进入社会圈。来自宇宙的信息肯定影响了人类对自己和环境的形象，如果我们假设我们生活在一个完全被云层覆盖的星球上，挡住了来自外部宇宙的所有信息，我们就可以很容易地想象到这一点。当然，直到最近，来自宇宙的信息才被捕获并编码成复杂图像的形式，显示宇宙在地球之外的样子；但即使在原始时代，人类对天体的感知也始终深刻地影响着他对地球和自身的形象。然而，地球内部产生的信息，特别是人类自己产生的信息，构成了信息系统的大部分。我们可以将知识储备，或者如德日进所说的，称为“智力圈”，并将其视为一个开放的系统，通过衰老和死亡失去知识，并通过出生、教育和日常生活经验获得知识。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S007")[#text("From the human point of view, knowledge or information is by far the most important of the three systems. Matter only acquires significance and only enters the sociosphere or the econosphere insofar as it becomes an object of human knowledge. We can think of capital, indeed, as frozen knowledge or knowledge imposed on the material world in the form of improbable arrangements. A machine, for instance, originated in the mind of man, and both its construction and its use involve information processes imposed on the material world by man himself. The cumulation of knowledge, that is, the excess of its production over its consumption, is the key to human development of all kinds, especially to economic development. We can see this pre-eminence of knowledge very clearly in the experiences of countries where the material capital has been destroyed by a war, as in Japan and Germany. The knowledge of the people was not destroyed, and it did not take long, therefore, certainly not more than ten years, for most of the material capital to be reestablished again. In a country such as Indonesia, however, where the knowledge did not exist, the material capital did not come into being either. By \"knowledge\" here I mean, of course, the whole cognitive structure, which includes valuations and motivations as well as images of the factual world.")]

#zh(id: "S007")[#text("从人类的角度来看，知识或信息是迄今为止三个系统中最重要的。物质只有成为人类知识的对象，才具有意义并进入社会圈或经济圈。事实上，我们可以将资本视为冻结的知识或以不可能的安排形式强加于物质世界的知识。例如，一台机器起源于人类的思想，它的构造和使用都涉及人类自身强加于物质世界的信息过程。知识的积累，即知识的生产超过其消费，是人类各种发展，特别是经济发展的关键。在物质资本被战争摧毁的国家（如日本和德国）的经验中，我们可以清楚地看到这种知识的卓越性。人们的知识并没有被摧毁，而且时间不长，因此，肯定不会超过十年，大部分物质资本就重新建立起来。然而，在印度尼西亚这样的国家，知识不存在，物质资本也不会形成。当然，这里的“知识”是指整个认知结构，包括评价和动机以及事实世界的图像。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S008")[#text("The concept of entropy, used in a somewhat loose sense, can be applied to all three of these open systems. In the case of material systems, we can distinguish between entropic processes, which take concentrated materials and diffuse them through the oceans or over the earth's surface or into the atmosphere, and anti-entropic processes, which take diffuse materials and concentrate them. Material entropy can be taken as a measure of the uniformity of the distribution of elements and, more uncertainly, compounds and other structures on the earth's surface. There is, fortunately, no law of increasing material entropy, as there is in the corresponding case of energy, as it is quite possible to concentrate diffused materials if energy inputs are allowed. Thus the processes for fixation of nitrogen from the air, processes for the extraction of magnesium or other elements from the sea, and processes for the desalinization of sea water are anti-entropic in the material sense, though the reduction of material entropy has to be paid for by inputs of energy and also inputs of information, or at least a stock of information in the system. In regard to matter, therefore, a closed system is conceivable, that is, a system in which there is neither increase nor decrease in material entropy. In such a system all outputs from consumption would constantly be recycled to become inputs for production, as for instance, nitrogen in the nitrogen cycle of the natural ecosystem.")]

#zh(id: "S008")[#text("熵的概念，在某种程度上宽松的意义上使用，可以应用于所有这三个开放系统。就材料系统而言，我们可以区分熵过程和反熵过程，熵过程采用集中材料并将其扩散到海洋或地球表面或大气中，反熵过程采用扩散材料并将其集中。物质熵可以作为地球表面元素分布均匀性的度量，更不确定的是化合物和其他结构的分布均匀性。幸运的是，不存在物质熵增加的规律，而在相应的能量情况下却存在这种规律，因为如果允许能量输入，则很可能集中扩散的材料。因此，从空气中固定氮的过程、从海洋中提取镁或其他元素的过程以及海水淡化的过程在物质意义上是反熵的，尽管物质熵的减少必须通过能量输入和信息输入或至少系统中的信息储备来支付。因此，就物质而言，封闭系统是可以想象的，即物质熵既不增加也不减少的系统。在这样的系统中，消费的所有产出都会不断地被回收，成为生产的投入，例如自然生态系统氮循环中的氮。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S009")[#text("In regard to the energy system there is, unfortunately, no escape from the grim Second Law of Thermodynamics; and if there were no energy inputs into the earth, any evolutionary or developmental process would be impossible. The large energy inputs which we have obtained from fossil fuels are strictly temporary. Even the most optimistic predictions would expect the easily available supply of fossil fuels to be exhausted in a mere matter of centuries at present rates of use. If the rest of the world were to rise to American standards of power consumption, and still more if world population continues to increase, the exhaustion of fossil fuels would be even more rapid. The development of nuclear energy has improved this picture, but has not fundamentally altered it, at least in present technologies, for fissionable material is still relatively scarce. If we should achieve the economic use of energy through fusion, of course, a much larger source of energy materials would be available, which would expand the time horizons of supplementary energy input into an open social system by perhaps tens to hundreds of thousands of years. Failing this, however, the time is not very far distant, historically speaking, when man will once more have to retreat to his current energy input from the sun, even though this could be used much more effectively than in the past with increased knowledge. Up to now, certainly, we have not got very far with the technology of using current solar energy, but the possibility of substantial improvements in the future is certainly high. It may be, indeed, that the biological revolution which is just beginning will produce a solution to this problem, as we develop artificial organisms which are capable of much more efficient transformation of solar energy into easily available forms than any that we now have. As Richard Meier has suggested, we may run our machines in the future with methane-producing algae.[2]")]

#zh(id: "S009")[#text("不幸的是，就能量系统而言，它无法逃脱严酷的热力学第二定律。如果没有能量输入地球，任何进化或发展过程都是不可能的。我们从化石燃料获得的大量能源输入完全是暂时的。即使是最乐观的预测也认为，以目前的使用速度，容易获得的化石燃料供应将在短短几个世纪内耗尽。如果世界其他地区的电力消耗达到美国的标准，而且如果世界人口继续增加，化石燃料的枯竭将会更快。核能的发展改善了这一情况，但并没有从根本上改变它，至少在现有技术中是这样，因为可裂变材料仍然相对稀缺。如果我们能够通过聚变实现能源的经济利用，当然，将会有更多的能源材料可供使用，这将把补充能源输入到开放社会系统的时间范围扩大到可能数万到数十万年。然而，如果做不到这一点，从历史上看，人类将不得不再次退回到目前来自太阳的能量输入的时间并不遥远，尽管随着知识的增加，这可以比过去更有效地利用。当然，到目前为止，我们在利用现有太阳能的技术方面还没有取得很大进展，但未来大幅改进的可能性肯定很高。事实上，刚刚开始的生物革命可能会解决这个问题，因为我们开发出能够更有效地转化太阳能的人造生物体。将能量转化为比我们现在拥有的任何形式都更容易获得的形式。正如理查德·迈耶（Richard Meier）所建议的，我们将来可能会用产生甲烷的藻类来运行我们的机器。 [2]")]

#html.p(id: "en-S010")[#text("The question of whether there is anything corresponding to entropy in the information system is a puzzling one, though of great interest. There are certainly many examples of social systems and cultures which have lost knowledge, especially in transition from one generation to the next, and in which the culture has therefore degenerated. One only has to look at the folk culture of Appalachian migrants to American cities to see a culture which started out as a fairly rich European folk culture in Elizabethan times and which seems to have lost both skills, adaptability, folk tales, songs, and almost everything that goes up to make richness and complexity in a culture, in the course of about ten generations. The American Indians on reservations provide another example of such degradation of the information and knowledge system. On the other hand, over a great part of human history, the growth of knowledge in the earth as a whole seems to have been almost continuous, even though there have been times of relatively slow growth and times of rapid growth. As it is knowledge of certain kinds that produces the growth of knowledge in general, we have here a very subtle and complicated system, and it is hard to put one's finger on the particular elements in a culture which make knowledge grow more or less rapidly, or even which make it decline. One of the great puzzles in this connection, for instance, is why the take-off into science, which represents an \"acceleration,\" or an increase in the rate of growth of knowledge in European society in the sixteenth century, did not take place in China, which at that time (about 1600) was unquestionably ahead of Europe, and one would think even more ready for the breakthrough. This is perhaps the most crucial question in the theory of social development, yet we must confess that it is very little understood. Perhaps the most significant factor in this connection is the existence of \"slack\" in the culture, which permits a divergence from established patterns and activity which is not merely devoted to reproducing the existing society but is devoted to changing it. China was perhaps too well-organized and had too little slack in its society to produce the kind of acceleration which we find in the somewhat poorer and less well-organized but more diverse societies of Europe.")]

#zh(id: "S010")[#text("信息系统中是否存在与熵相对应的东西是一个令人费解的问题，尽管它很有趣。当然，有很多社会制度和文化失去了知识的例子，特别是在一代又一代的过渡过程中，文化因此而退化。只要看看阿巴拉契亚移民到美国城市的民间文化，就会发现这种文化最初是伊丽莎白时代相当丰富的欧洲民间文化，但在大约十代人的过程中，这种文化似乎已经失去了技能、适应性、民间故事、歌曲以及几乎所有构成文化丰富性和复杂性的东西。保留地的美洲印第安人提供了信息和知识系统退化的另一个例子。另一方面，在人类历史的很大一部分时间里，整个地球上的知识增长似乎几乎是连续的，尽管有过相对缓慢增长的时期和快速增长的时期。由于正是某些种类的知识导致了一般知识的增长，因此我们这里有一个非常微妙和复杂的系统，很难指出文化中哪些特定因素使知识或多或少快速增长，甚至导致知识衰退。例如，这方面的一大难题是，为什么科学的起飞没有发生在中国，科学的起飞代表着16世纪欧洲社会的“加速”或知识增长速度的提高，而当时的中国（大约1600年）毫无疑问领先于欧洲，人们会认为中国已经为突破做好了更充分的准备。这或许是理论中最关键的问题社会发展，但我们必须承认，人们对它知之甚少。也许这方面最重要的因素是文化中存在的“松弛”，它允许与既定模式和活动的分歧，这些模式和活动不仅致力于复制现有社会，而且致力于改变它。中国或许组织得太严密，社会闲暇太少，无法产生我们在欧洲较为贫穷、组织欠佳但更加多元化的社会中看到的那种加速。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S011")[#text("The closed earth of the future requires economic principles which are somewhat different from those of the open earth of the past. For the sake of picturesqueness, I am tempted to call the open economy the \"cowboy economy,\" the cowboy being symbolic of the illimitable plains and also associated with reckless, exploitative, romantic, and violent behavior, which is characteristic of open societies. The closed economy of the future might similarly be called the \"spaceman\" economy, in which the earth has become a single spaceship, without unlimited reservoirs of anything, either for extraction or for pollution, and in which, therefore, man must find his place in a cyclical ecological system which is capable of continuous reproduction of material form even though it cannot escape having inputs of energy. The difference between the two types of economy becomes most apparent in the attitude towards consumption. In the cowboy economy, consumption is regarded as a good thing and production likewise; and the success of the economy is measured by the amount of the throughput from the \"factors of production,\" a part of which, at any rate, is extracted from the reservoirs of raw materials and noneconomic objects, and another part of which is output into the reservoirs of pollution. If there are infinite reservoirs from which material can be obtained and into which effluvia can be deposited, then the throughput is at least a plausible measure of the success of the economy. The gross national product is a rough measure of this total throughput. It should be possible, however, to distinguish that part of the GNP which is derived from exhaustible and that which is derived from reproducible resources, as well as that part of consumption which represents effluvia and that which represents input into the productive system again. Nobody, as far as I know, has ever attempted to break down the GNP in this way, although it would be an interesting and extremely important exercise, which is unfortunately beyond the scope of this paper.")]

#zh(id: "S011")[#text("未来的封闭地球所需要的经济原则与过去开放地球的经济原则有些不同。为了形象起见，我很想把开放经济称为“牛仔经济”，牛仔象征着无边无际的平原，也与鲁莽、剥削、浪漫和暴力行为联系在一起，这是开放社会的特征。未来的封闭经济同样可以被称为“太空人”经济，在这种经济中，地球变成了一艘单一的宇宙飞船，没有无限的储存任何东西的能力，无论是用于开采还是用于污染，因此，人类必须在一个循环的生态系统中找到自己的位置，这个生态系统能够持续繁殖物质形式，尽管它无法逃脱能量的输入。两种经济类型之间的差异在消费态度上表现得最为明显。在牛仔经济中，消费被视为好事，生产也被视为好事；经济的成功是通过“生产要素”的吞吐量来衡量的，无论如何，其中一部分是从原材料和非经济物品的储备中提取的，另一部分则输出到污染的储备中。如果有无数个可以从中获取材料并可以将废气沉积到其中的水库，那么吞吐量至少是经济成功的一个合理衡量标准。国民生产总值是对总吞吐量的粗略衡量。然而，应该可以区分国民生产总值中来自可耗竭资源的部分和来自可再生资源的部分，以及代表废气的消费部分和来自可再生资源的消费部分。代表再次输入生产系统。据我所知，没有人试图以这种方式分解国民生产总值，尽管这将是一个有趣且极其重要的练习，但不幸的是超出了本文的范围。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S012")[#text("By contrast, in the spaceman economy, throughput is by no means a desideratum, and is indeed to be regarded as something to be minimized rather than maximized. The essential measure of the success of the economy is not production and consumption at all, but the nature, extent, quality, and complexity of the total capital stock, including in this the state of the human bodies and minds included in the system. In the spaceman economy, what we are primarily concerned with is stock maintenance, and any technological change which results in the maintenance of a given total stock with a lessened throughput (that is, less production and consumption) is clearly a gain. This idea that both production and consumption are bad things rather than good things is very strange to economists, who have been obsessed with the income-flow concepts to the exclusion, almost, of capital-stock concepts.")]

#zh(id: "S012")[#text("相比之下，在太空人经济中，吞吐量绝不是一个迫切需要的东西，而且确实应该被视为需要最小化而不是最大化。经济成功的基本衡量标准根本不是生产和消费，而是总资本存量的性质、范围、质量和复杂性，其中包括系统中人类的身体和思想的状态。在太空人经济中，我们主要关心的是库存维持，任何导致在减少吞吐量（即减少生产和消耗）的情况下维持给定总库存的技术变革显然都是一种收益。这种认为生产和消费都是坏事而不是好事的想法对于经济学家来说是非常奇怪的，因为他们一直痴迷于收入流概念而几乎排除了资本存量概念。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S013")[#text("There are actually some very tricky and unsolved problems involved in the questions as to whether human welfare or well- being is to be regarded as a stock or a flow. Something of both these elements seems actually to be involved in it, and as far as I know there have been practically no studies directed towards identifying these two dimensions of human satisfaction. Is it, for instance, eating that is a good thing, or is it being well fed? Does economic welfare involve having nice clothes, fine houses, good equipment, and so on, or is it to be measured by the depreciation and the wearing out of these things? I am inclined myself to regard the stock concept as most fundamental, that is, to think of being well fed as more important than eating, and to think even of so-called services as essentially involving the restoration of a depleting psychic capital. Thus I have argued that we go to a concert in order to restore a psychic condition which might be called \"just having gone to a concert,\" which, once established, tends to depreciate. When it depreciates beyond a certain point, we go to another concert in order to restore it. If it depreciates rapidly, we go to a lot of concerts; if it depreciates slowly, we go to few. On this view, similarly, we eat primarily to restore bodily homeostasis, that is, to maintain a condition of being well fed, and so on. On this view, there is nothing desirable in consumption at all. The less consumption we can maintain a given state with, the better off we are. If we had clothes that did not wear out, houses that did not depreciate, and even if we could maintain our bodily condition without eating, we would clearly be much better off.")]

#zh(id: "S013")[#text("在人类福利或福祉到底应该被视为存量还是流量的问题中，实际上存在着一些非常棘手且尚未解决的问题。这两个要素似乎实际上都参与其中，据我所知，实际上还没有任何研究旨在确定人类满意度的这两个维度。例如，吃东西是好事还是吃得好？经济福利是指拥有漂亮的衣服、漂亮的房子、好的设备等等，还是用这些东西的折旧和磨损来衡量？我倾向于将存量概念视为最根本的概念，即认为吃饱比吃饭更重要，甚至认为所谓的服务本质上涉及到耗尽的精神资本的恢复。因此，我认为我们去听音乐会是为了恢复一种精神状态，这种状态可以被称为“只是去听一场音乐会”，这种状态一旦建立，就会贬值。当它贬值超过某个点时，我们会去另一场音乐会以恢复它。如果人民币贬值很快，我们就会去很多音乐会；如果它贬值缓慢，我们就很少。同样，按照这种观点，我们吃东西主要是为了恢复身体的稳态，即维持吃得饱的状态，等等。从这个角度来看，消费根本没有什么可取之处。我们维持特定状态的消耗越少，我们的生活就越好。如果我们有穿不破的衣服，不贬值的房子，甚至不吃东西也能维持身体状况，显然会过得更好。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S014")[#text("It is this last consideration, perhaps, which makes one pause. Would we, for instance, really want an operation that would enable us to restore all our bodily tissues by intravenous feeding while we slept? Is there not, that is to say, a certain virtue in throughput itself, in activity itself, in production and consumption itself, in raising food and in eating it? It would certainly be rash to exclude this possibility. Further interesting problems are raised by the demand for variety. We certainly do not want a constant state to be maintained; we want fluctuations in the state. Otherwise there would be no demand for variety in food, for variety in scene, as in travel, for variety in social contact, and so on. The demand for variety can, of course, be costly, and sometimes it seems to be too costly to be tolerated or at least legitimated, as in the case of marital partners, where the maintenance of a homeostatic state in the family is usually regarded as much more desirable than the variety and excessive throughput of the libertine. There are problems here which the economics profession has neglected with astonishing singlemindedness. My own attempts to call attention to some of them, for instance, in two articles[3], as far as I can judge, produced no response whatever; and economists continue to think and act as if production, consumption, throughput, and the GNP were the sufficient and adequate measure of economic success.")]

#zh(id: "S014")[#text("也许正是这最后一个考虑因素让我们停下来。例如，我们真的想要一项手术，使我们能够在睡觉时通过静脉注射来恢复所有的身体组织吗？也就是说，生产量本身、活动本身、生产和消费本身、饲养食物和食用食物本身不存在某种美德吗？排除这种可能性肯定是草率的。对多样性的需求引发了更多有趣的问题。我们当然不希望维持一个恒定的状态；我们希望国家出现波动。否则，就不会需要多样化的食物、多样化的场景（如旅行）、多样化的社交接触等等。当然，对多样性的需求可能会付出高昂的代价，有时似乎成本太高而无法容忍或至少是合法的，就像婚姻伴侣的情况一样，在家庭中维持稳态通常被认为比浪荡子的多样性和过度生产更可取。这里存在一些问题，而经济学界却一心一意地忽视了这些问题。据我判断，我自己在两篇文章[3]中试图引起人们对其中一些问题的关注，但没有产生任何回应；经济学家继续认为生产、消费、吞吐量和国民生产总值是经济成功的充分衡量标准。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S015")[#text("It may be said, of course, why worry about all this when the spaceman economy is still a good way off (at least beyond the lifetimes of any now living), so let us eat, drink, spend, extract and pollute, and be as merry as we can, and let posterity worry about the spaceship earth. It is always a little hard to find a convincing answer to the man who says, \"What has posterity ever done for me?\" and the conservationist has always had to fall back on rather vague ethical principles postulating identity of the individual with some human community or society which extends not only back into the past but forward into the future. Unless the individual identifies with some community of this kind, conservation is obviously \"irrational.\" Why should we not maximize the welfare of this generation at the cost of posterity? \"Après nous, le deluge\" has been the motto of not insignificant numbers of human societies. The only answer to this, as far as I can see, is to point out that the welfare of the individual depends on the extent to which he can identify himself with others, and that the most satisfactory individual identity is that which identifies not only with a community in space but also with a community extending over time from the past into the future. If this kind of identity is recognized as desirable, then posterity has a voice, even if it does not have a vote; and in a sense, if its voice can influence votes, it has votes too. This whole problem is linked up with the much larger one of the determinants of the morale, legitimacy, and \"nerve\" of a society, and there is a great deal of historical evidence to suggest that a society which loses its identity with posterity and which loses its positive image of the future loses also its capacity to deal with present problems, and soon falls apart.[4] Even if we concede that posterity is relevant to our present problems, we still face the question of time-discounting and the closely related question of uncertainty-discounting. It is a well- known phenomenon that individuals discount the future, even in their own lives. The very existence of a positive rate of interest may be taken as at least strong supporting evidence of this hypothesis. If we discount our own future, it is certainly not unreasonable to discount posterity's future even more, even if we do give posterity a vote. If we discount this at 5 per cent per annum, posterity's vote or dollar halves every fourteen years as we look into the future, and after even a mere hundred years it is pretty small - only about 1 and 1/2 cents on the dollar. If we add another 5 per cent for uncertainty, even the vote of our grandchildren reduces almost to insignificance. We can argue, of course, that the ethical thing to do is not to discount the future at all, that time-discounting is mainly the result of myopia and perspective, and hence is an illusion which the moral man should not tolerate. It is a very popular illusion, however, and one that must certainly be taken into consideration in the formulation of policies. It explains, perhaps, why conservationist policies almost have to be sold under some other excuse which seems more urgent, and why, indeed, necessities which are visualized as urgent, such as defense, always seem to hold priority over those which involve the future.")]

#zh(id: "S015")[#text("当然，也许有人会说，既然太空人经济还很遥远（至少超出了任何现在活着的人的有生之年），为什么还要担心这一切呢？所以让我们吃、喝、消费、提取和污染，尽可能地快乐，让子孙后代担心宇宙飞船地球。当有人问“子孙后代为我做了什么？”时，要找到令人信服的答案总是有点困难。自然资源保护主义者总是不得不依靠相当模糊的道德原则，假定个人与某个人类社区或社会的身份，这种身份不仅可以追溯到过去，而且可以延伸到未来。除非个人认同此类社区，否则保护显然是“非理性的”。为什么我们不应该以牺牲子孙后代为代价来实现这一代人的福利最大化呢？ “Après nous, le deluge”一直是不少人类社会的座右铭。据我所知，对此问题的唯一答案是指出个人的福利取决于他能够在多大程度上认同他人，而最令人满意的个人身份不仅是空间上的共同体，而且是随着时间的推移从过去延伸到未来的共同体。如果这种身份被认为是可取的，那么后代即使没有投票权，也有发言权；从某种意义上说，如果它的声音能够影响选票，那么它也有选票。整个问题与一个社会的士气、合法性和“勇气”的更大决定因素联系在一起，并且有大量历史证据表明，一个社会如果失去了后世的认同，失去了对未来的积极形象，也就失去了处理当前问题的能力，很快就会分崩离析。[4]即使我们承认后代与我们当前的问题有关，我们仍然面临时间贴现问题和密切相关的不确定性贴现问题。一个众所周知的现象是，人们对未来（甚至在他们自己的生活中）的未来不予考虑。正利率的存在至少可以被视为这一假设的有力支持证据。如果我们对自己的未来打折扣，那么对子孙后代的未来打折扣当然也不是没有道理的，即使我们确实给予子孙后代投票权。如果我们以每年 5% 的利率贴现，那么当我们展望未来时，后人的选票或美元每十四年就会减半，即使在仅仅一百年之后，它也相当小——大约只有 1 又 1/2 美分。如果我们再加上 5% 的不确定性，即使是我们的孙辈的投票也几乎变得微不足道。当然，我们可以说，道德上要做的事情根本不是对未来打折，时间打折主要是近视和视角的结果，因此是有道德的人不应该容忍的幻想。然而，这是一种非常普遍的幻想，在制定政策时必须考虑到这一幻想。或许，它解释了为什么保护主义政策几乎必须以其他一些似乎更紧迫的借口来兜售，以及为什么事实上，被认为是紧迫的必需品（例如国防）似乎总是优先于那些涉及未来的需求。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S016")[#text("All these considerations add some credence to the point of view which says that we should not worry about the spaceman economy at all, and that we should just go on increasing the GNP and indeed the gross world product, or GWP, in the expectation that the problems of the future can be left to the future, that when scarcities arise, whether this is of raw materials or of pollutable reservoirs, the needs of the then present will determine the solutions of the then present, and there is no use giving ourselves ulcers by worrying about problems that we really do not have to solve. There is even high ethical authority for this point of view in the New Testament, which advocates that we should take no thought for tomorrow and let the dead bury their dead. There has always been something rather refreshing in the view that we should live like the birds, and perhaps posterity is for the birds in more senses than one; so perhaps we should all call it a day and go out and pollute something cheerfully. As an old taker of thought for the morrow, however, I cannot quite accept this solution; and I would argue, furthermore, that tomorrow is not only very close, but in many respects it is already here. The shadow of the future spaceship, indeed, is already falling over our spendthrift merriment. Oddly enough, it seems to be in pollution rather than in exhaustion that the problem is first becoming salient.")]

#zh(id: "S016")[#text("所有这些考虑都增加了这样一种观点的可信度：我们根本不应该担心太空人经济，我们应该继续增加国民生产总值，实际上是世界生产总值，或GWP，期望未来的问题可以留给未来，当出现短缺时，无论是原材料还是可污染的水库，当时的需求将决定当时的解决方案，担心未来的问题给自己带来溃疡是没有用的。我们确实没有必要解决。这种观点在《新约》中甚至有很高的伦理权威，主张我们不要为明天而忧虑，让死者埋葬他们的死者。我们应该像鸟儿一样生活，这一观点总是令人耳目一新，也许后代对于鸟儿来说有更多的意义；所以也许我们都应该到此为止，愉快地出去污染一些东西。然而，作为一个经常思考明天的人，我不能完全接受这个解决方案。此外，我还认为，明天不仅已经临近，而且在许多方面它已经到来了。事实上，未来宇宙飞船的阴影已经笼罩在我们挥霍无度的欢乐之上。奇怪的是，问题似乎是在污染而不是疲惫中首先变得突出的。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S017")[#text("Los Angeles has run out of air, Lake Erie has become a cesspool, the oceans are getting full of lead and DDT, and the atmosphere may become man's major problem in another generation, at the rate at which we are filling it up with gunk. It is, of course, true that at least on a microscale, things have been worse at times in the past. The cities of today, with all their foul air and polluted waterways, are probably not as bad as the filthy cities of the pretechnical age. Nevertheless, that fouling of the nest which has been typical of man's activity in the past on a local scale now seems to be extending to the whole world society; and one certainly cannot view with equanimity the present rate of pollution of any of the natural reservoirs, whether the atmosphere, the lakes, or even the oceans.")]

#zh(id: "S017")[#text("洛杉矶的空气已经耗尽，伊利湖已经变成了污水池，海洋中充满了铅和滴滴涕，而大气层可能会成为下一代人面临的主要问题，以我们正在用粘稠物填满大气层的速度而言。当然，至少在微观层面上，过去的情况确实有时会更糟。今天的城市空气污浊，水道受污染，可能并不像前技术时代肮脏的城市那么糟糕。然而，过去在局部范围内人类活动所特有的对巢穴的污染现在似乎正在扩展到整个世界社会；人们当然无法平静地看待任何天然水库目前的污染速度，无论是大气、湖泊还是海洋。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S018")[#text("I would argue strongly also that our obsession with production and consumption to the exclusion of the \"state\" aspects of human welfare distorts the process of technological change in a most undesirable way. We are all familiar, of course, with the wastes involved in planned obsolescence, in competitive advertising, and in poor quality of consumer goods. These problems may not be so important as the \"view with alarm\" school indicates, and indeed the evidence at many points is conflicting. New materials especially seem to edge towards the side of improved durability, such as, for instance, neolite soles for footwear, nylon socks, wash and wear shirts, and so on. The case of household equipment and automobiles is a little less clear. Housing and building construction generally almost certainly has declined in durability since the Middle Ages, but this decline also reflects a change in tastes towards flexibility and fashion and a need for novelty, so that it is not easy to assess. What is clear is that no serious attempt has been made to assess the impact over the whole of economic life of changes in durability, that is, in the ratio of capital in the widest possible sense to income. I suspect that we have underestimated, even in our spendthrift society, the gains from increased durability, and that this might very well be one of the places where the price system needs correction through government-sponsored research and development. The problems which the spaceship earth is going to present, therefore, are not all in the future by any means, and a strong case can be made for paying much more attention to them in the present than we now do.")]

#zh(id: "S018")[#text("我还强烈主张，我们对生产和消费的痴迷而排除了人类福利的“国家”方面，从而以一种最不受欢迎的方式扭曲了技术变革的进程。当然，我们都熟悉计划报废、竞争性广告和劣质消费品所造成的浪费。这些问题可能并不像“警惕的观点”学派所指出的那么重要，而且事实上，许多方面的证据是相互矛盾的。新材料似乎特别倾向于提高耐用性，例如用于鞋类的 Neolite 鞋底、尼龙袜子、洗涤和穿着衬衫等。家用设备和汽车的情况不太清楚。几乎可以肯定，自中世纪以来，住房和建筑的耐用性普遍下降，但这种下降也反映了人们对灵活性和时尚的品味的变化以及对新颖性的需求，因此不容易评估。显而易见的是，我们并没有认真地尝试评估耐久性（即最广泛意义上的资本与收入之比）的变化对整个经济生活的影响。我怀疑，即使在我们挥霍无度的社会中，我们也低估了耐用性提高所带来的收益，而且这很可能是价格体系需要通过政府资助的研究和开发进行修正的地方之一。因此，地球飞船将要提出的问题无论如何都不会出现在未来，并且有充分的理由比我们现在对这些问题给予更多的关注。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S019")[#text("It may be complained that the considerations I have been putting forth relate only to the very long run, and they do not much concern our immediate problems. There may be some justice in this criticism, and my main excuse is that other writers have dealt adequately with the more immediate problems of deterioration in the quality of the environment. It is true, for instance, that many of the immediate problems of pollution of the atmosphere or of bodies of water arise because of the failure of the price system, and many of them could be solved by corrective taxation. If people had to pay the losses due to the nuisances which they create, a good deal more resources would go into the prevention of nuisances. These arguments involving external economies and diseconomies are familiar to economists, and there is no teed to recapitulate them. The law of torts is quite inadequate to provide for the correction of the price system which is required, simply because where damages are widespread and their incidence on any particular person is small, the ordinary remedies of the civil law are quite inadequate and inappropriate. There needs, therefore, to be special legislation to cover these cases, and though such legislation seems hard to get in practice, mainly because of the widespread and small personal incidence of the injuries, the technical problems involved are not insuperable. If we were to adopt in principle a law for tax penalties for social damages, with an apparatus for making assessments under it, a very large proportion of current pollution and deterioration of the environment would be prevented. There are tricky problems of equity involved, particularly where old established nuisances create a kind of \"right by purchase\" to perpetuate themselves, but these are problems again which a few rather arbitrary decisions can bring to some kind of solution. The problems which I have been raising in this paper are of larger scale and perhaps much harder to solve than the more practical and immediate problems of the above paragraph. Our success in dealing with the larger problems, however, is not unrelated to the development of skill in the solution of the more immediate and perhaps less difficult problems. One can hope, therefore, that as a succession of mounting crises, especially in pollution, arouse public opinion and mobilize support for the solution of the immediate problems, a learning process will be set in motion which will eventually lead to an appreciation of and perhaps solutions for the larger ones. My neglect of the immediate problems, therefore, is in no way intended to deny their importance, for unless we at least make a beginning on a process for solving the immediate problems we will not have much chance of solving the larger ones. On the other hand, it may also be true that a long-run vision, as it were, of the deep crisis which faces mankind may predispose people to taking more interest in the immediate problems and to devote more effort for their solution. This may sound like a rather modest optimism, but perhaps a modest optimism is better than no optimism at all.")]

#zh(id: "S019")[#text("也许有人会抱怨，我所提出的考虑只涉及很长远的问题，与我们眼前的问题关系不大。这种批评可能有一定道理，我的主要借口是其他作家已经充分处理了环境质量恶化这一更紧迫的问题。例如，确实，许多直接的大气污染或水体污染问题是由于价格体系的失败而产生的，其中许多问题可以通过纠正性税收来解决。如果人们必须为自己造成的滋扰所造成的损失付出代价，那么就会有更多的资源用于预防滋扰。这些涉及外部经济和不经济的论点对于经济学家来说是熟悉的，并且没有必要重述它们。侵权法不足以对价格体系进行所需的纠正，这仅仅是因为在损害广泛且对任何特定人的影响很小的情况下，民法的普通补救措施就相当不足和不适当。因此，需要有专门的立法来涵盖这些案件，虽然这种立法似乎很难在实践中实施，主要是因为伤害的普遍性和较小的个人发生率，但所涉及的技术问题并非不可克服。如果我们原则上通过一项对社会损害进行税收处罚的法律，并在其下建立评估机构，那么当前的污染和环境恶化在很大程度上就可以避免。这里涉及到棘手的公平问题，特别是当旧有的滋扰创造了一种“购买权”来延续自己的情况时，但这些都是一些相当随意的决定可以带来某种解决方案的问题再次出现。我在本文中提出的问题比上一段中更实际、更紧迫的问题规模更大，而且可能更难解决。然而，我们在处理更大问题上的成功与解决更直接、或许不太困难的问题的技能发展并非没有关系。因此，人们可以希望，随着一系列不断加剧的危机，特别是污染问题，激起公众舆论并动员对解决眼前问题的支持，一个学习过程将启动，最终将导致对更大问题的认识，或许还会找到解决方案。因此，我对眼前问题的忽视绝不是为了否认它们的重要性，因为除非我们至少开始解决眼前问题，否则我们将没有太多机会解决更大的问题。另一方面，对人类面临的深刻危机的长期愿景也可能使人们对眼前的问题更加感兴趣，并为解决这些问题付出更多的努力。这听起来可能是一种相当温和的乐观态度，但也许适度的乐观态度比完全不乐观要好。")]

